---
import PerformanceCase from "./PerformanceCase.astro";
import PipelineTerminal from "./PipelineTerminal.astro";

/**
 * Field data, Vercel Speed Insights, P75 desktop on UAT, taken before the
 * optimization pass. `good` is the Core Web Vitals threshold, not my target.
 * `plain` is the same wording WebVitals.astro uses, so the two strips can be
 * read side by side by someone who does not know the acronyms.
 */
const FIELD_METRICS = [
  { name: "LCP", plain: "main content appears", value: "3.92s", good: "< 2.5s", pass: false },
  { name: "FCP", plain: "page starts drawing", value: "2.55s", good: "< 1.8s", pass: false },
  { name: "TTFB", plain: "server answers", value: "1.51s", good: "< 0.8s", pass: false },
  { name: "INP", plain: "taps respond", value: "112ms", good: "< 200ms", pass: true },
  { name: "CLS", plain: "layout holds still", value: "0.01", good: "< 0.1", pass: true },
];

const ROOT_CAUSES = [
  {
    cause: "An App Router app rendering almost entirely on the client",
    effect:
      "~270 files carry a use-client directive, so data only starts fetching after hydrate — LCP waits on a chain of download, hydrate, call, render.",
  },
  {
    cause: "revalidate = 0 on the root layout",
    effect:
      "Forces a dynamic render on every request and switches off the Full Route Cache, so TTFB cannot come down however well the client behaves.",
  },
  {
    cause: "Heavy libraries imported statically",
    effect:
      "Editor, document viewer, spreadsheet and charts land in the initial bundle, with the animation libraries riding along on every route.",
  },
  {
    cause: "Duplicate libraries doing the same job",
    effect:
      "echarts alongside recharts, dayjs alongside date-fns, firebase compat — double the bytes for none of the value.",
  },
];
---

<section
  id="performance"
  class="container mx-auto mb-28 scroll-mt-24 p-6"
  aria-labelledby="performance-title"
>
  <h2 id="performance-title" class="section-title">Performance</h2>

  <p class="mt-8 mb-6 max-w-3xl text-lg text-secondary md:text-2xl">
    A page in SAPP&apos;s learning platform took 15 seconds to open. Without the
    jargon: I measured why, got it down to 6, and one fix that worked is still
    deliberately not in production. Slow pages lose learners, so this is the work
    I&apos;d want to be judged on.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-8 max-w-3xl text-secondary">
    The pass covered 15 routes. Drag the slider to replay it — the scores are
    the audit, not a marketing round-up. Further down is the change I measured,
    proved out, and then chose not to ship; that is the one I&apos;d rather talk
    about.
  </p>

  <PerformanceCase />

  <h3 class="heading mt-14 mb-4 text-2xl uppercase md:text-3xl">Reading the numbers before touching the code</h3>
  <p class="mb-5 max-w-[68ch]">
    Field data first, so the work follows real users rather than a lab score.
    Thresholds are the Core Web Vitals targets — Google&apos;s industry-standard
    pass mark for each one. A crossed box means most users were getting worse
    than that; a filled box means it passed.
  </p>

  <ul class="mb-6 grid grid-cols-2 border-t border-l border-dashed border-secondary sm:grid-cols-5">
    {
      FIELD_METRICS.map((metric) => (
        <li class="border-r border-b border-dashed border-secondary px-3 py-3">
          <p class="label flex items-center gap-2">
            <span
              class:list={[
                "h-2.5 w-2.5 shrink-0 border border-dashed border-secondary",
                metric.pass ? "bg-foreground" : "bg-background",
              ]}
              aria-hidden="true"
            />
            {metric.name}
          </p>
          {/* Reserve two lines so the values stay aligned across the row. */}
          <p class="mt-1 min-h-8 text-xs leading-tight text-secondary">
            {metric.plain}
          </p>
          <p
            class:list={[
              "font-mono text-xl font-bold tabular-nums",
              metric.pass ? "text-pass" : "text-fail",
            ]}
          >
            {metric.value}
          </p>
          <p class="font-mono text-xs tabular-nums text-secondary">{metric.good}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p class="mb-8 max-w-[68ch]">
    Every loading metric failed and both interaction metrics passed. That
    combination is the whole diagnosis: the app is bound by
    <strong class="font-semibold text-foreground">loading</strong>, not by
    interactivity or layout stability. So re-render and animation work — the
    usual reflex — went to the bottom of the queue, and server rendering, bundle
    size, CSS and images went to the top.
  </p>

  <p class="label mb-3 text-secondary">Four causes underneath it</p>
  <ol class="mb-12 border-l-2 border-dashed border-secondary">
    {
      ROOT_CAUSES.map((item, index) => (
        <li class="border-b border-dashed border-secondary px-5 py-3 last:border-b-0">
          <span class="mr-2 font-mono text-xs font-bold text-secondary tabular-nums">
            {String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
          </span>
          <span class="font-semibold text-foreground">{item.cause}.</span>{" "}
          <span class="text-secondary">{item.effect}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <h3 class="heading mb-4 text-2xl uppercase md:text-3xl">The fix I measured, then deliberately did not ship</h3>
  <div class="panel overflow-hidden">
    <p class="border-b border-dashed border-secondary bg-primary px-6 py-5 text-lg">
      In plain terms: it made one server-side number look dramatically better
      without making the page feel any faster to a single user. So it sits in a
      branch until the work that has to come first is done. The detail, for
      anyone who wants to check it:
    </p>
    <div class="space-y-4 px-6 py-6 text-secondary">
      <p>
        Dropping <code class="font-semibold text-foreground">revalidate = 0</code> and
        wrapping the provider tree in
        <code class="font-semibold text-foreground">&lt;Suspense&gt;</code> let 20 routes
        static-generate. TTFB on the main routes fell from roughly 60ms to 3ms
        locally, with a year-long <code class="font-semibold text-foreground">s-maxage</code>
        and a cache hit. The mechanism worked exactly as expected.
      </p>
      <p>
        It stayed in the working tree anyway. The static HTML is only a shell —
        <code class="font-semibold text-foreground">useSearchParams()</code> sits at the
        top of the provider tree, so the whole thing bails out to client-side
        rendering and the real content still arrives after hydrate. TTFB moves;
        LCP does not. On top of that, a shell cached at the CDN has to be
        provably free of user-specific data before it can go anywhere near
        production.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is a good change in the wrong order. It ships after the client
        boundary moves down to the leaves and the critical pages fetch on the
        server — otherwise it is a 15× win on a number no user feels.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="heading mt-14 mb-4 text-2xl uppercase md:text-3xl">Keeping it fast, and shut</h3>
  <p class="mb-6 max-w-[68ch]">
    A one-off audit decays in a sprint, so performance is tracked per route per
    release rather than re-discovered. The same pipeline blocks leaked secrets,
    vulnerable dependencies and insecure patterns before review ever sees them —
    in plain terms, every code change is automatically checked for passwords
    committed by accident, libraries with known security holes, risky code, and
    problems visible on the running site, and the change is blocked until it is
    clean.
  </p>

  <PipelineTerminal />

  <p class="mt-5">
    <a class="link" href="/writing/so-tay-appsec-cho-frontend-gitleaks-trivy-semgrep">
      I wrote the whole setup up as an AppSec handbook →
    </a>
  </p>
</section>
